          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "scan_url(url) is a call-graph-root (EntryPoint) whose `url` parameter is fully attacker-controlled and flows unsanitized into _http_get(url): urllib.parse.urlparse(url) feeds parsed_url.netloc directly into http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc) and parsed_url.path/query directly into conn.request(\"GET\", path_and_query), with no host/scheme allowlist. On a 302 response, _http_get recursively calls itself with response.headers[\"Location\"] (attacker/server-controlled) with no depth limit and no re-validation, so a malicious/compromised server can redirect the scanner to arbitrary internal hosts. This is a Server-Side Request Forgery sink: any caller that passes user-supplied input into scan_url (e.g. a web service letting users submit a model URL to scan) lets the attacker make the scanning host issue HTTPS connections to arbitrary network destinations, chained through unlimited redirects.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from the real source (not the analyst's say-so): src/picklescan/scanner.py:240-259 defines `_http_get(url)` which calls `urllib.parse.urlparse(url)`, passes `parsed_url.netloc` straight into `http.client.HTTPSConnection(...)` and `parsed_url.path`/`query` straight into `conn.request(\"GET\", path_and_query)` with zero host/scheme allowlisting anywhere in the module. On `response.status == 302` it recurses as `_http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])` with no depth limit and no re-validation of the new target \u2014 so a malicious or compromised HTTPS endpoint can redirect the scanning process to any other host:port (including internal/cloud-metadata addresses) and the second request is issued with the exact same unchecked logic. `scan_url(url)` (line 608-611) is a thin passthrough: `return scan_bytes(io.BytesIO(_http_get(url)), url)` \u2014 the `url` parameter flows unmodified into the vulnerable sink. cli.py:66 shows one real caller passing attacker-controllable input (`args.url`) straight through, confirming the parameter is meant to carry external input end-to-end. Graph queries against scan_id 4dfaf35be7a5414a53401ca970d5b6beae8672cc confirm: scan_url is a graph root (EntryPoint ep.method_full_name='...scan_url', hop_distance 0) and every call in the chain (urlparse\u2192HTTPSConnection, urlparse\u2192conn.request, getresponse\u2192_http_get(Location)) is reachable_from_entry=true with real FLOWS_TO edges exactly as the analyst described. Note: the EntryPoint 'handler' label here is applied broadly to nearly every top-level function in the module (including private helpers like _http_get itself) so it only establishes call-graph-root status, not network-handler status by itself \u2014 but the claim text correctly frames it only as \"call-graph-root\", and the actual SSRF/open-redirect-chaining mechanism is fully verified in source independent of that label. exploit_search returned no indexed corpus (not a Metasploit-catalog-type finding, which is expected for this kind of library-level SSRF) so that check is inconclusive/inapplicable, not contradicting."
          },
