        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-693",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "picklescan's own pass/fail verdict (and the CLI exit code CI pipelines rely on) only counts globals classified SafetyLevel.Dangerous toward issues_count; globals that are neither in the _safe_globals allowlist nor the _unsafe_globals denylist fall into SafetyLevel.Suspicious and are added to the globals list but never increment issues_count. Since cli.py's main() computes `sys.exit(0 if scan_result.issues_count == 0 else 1)`, a pickle/pytorch file whose __reduce__ target is an as-yet-unlisted (novel) module/function is reported only as 'suspicious' in the (opt-in, -g flag) globals listing but makes the overall scan exit 0 / 'no issues' \u2014 the standard control of 'fail closed on anything not explicitly vetted safe' is absent from the actual gating decision, even though the classifier itself defaults unknowns to Suspicious rather than Innocuous.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived directly from source, independent of the analyst. In scanner.py's `_build_scan_result_from_raw_globals` (lines 358-393), each raw global resolved from a pickle's GLOBAL/STACK_GLOBAL opcode is classified: \"unknown\" in module/name -> Dangerous (issues_count+=1, line 379-382); match in the hardcoded `_unsafe_globals` denylist -> Dangerous (issues_count+=1, line 383-386); match in `_safe_globals` allowlist -> Innocuous (line 387-388, no counter touched, correct since it's vetted-safe); else (not in either list) -> `g.safety = SafetyLevel.Suspicious` (line 389-390) with NO increment to issues_count. ScanResult.merge() (line 46-51) only sums issues_count, never touching Suspicious globals either. cli.py's main() (line 76) computes `return 0 if scan_result.issues_count == 0 else 1`, and `sys.exit(main())` (line 83) is invoked via the registered console-script entry point `picklescan = picklescan.cli:main` (confirmed in setup.cfg line 32) -- i.e., this is the actual, real-world CLI entry a CI pipeline runs. The `_unsafe_globals`/`_safe_globals` dicts (lines 66-188) are small, hand-maintained, finite lists, so any `__reduce__`/GLOBAL target referencing a module/function not yet enumerated there is classified Suspicious and is silently excluded from the exit-code decision -- the scan exits 0 (\"no issues\") even though a genuinely novel dangerous callable was present and is only visible via the opt-in `-g/--globals` listing. This is a real, exploitable gap in the tool's own primary security gate, not a graph artifact: the graph's `reachable_from_entry=false` on cli.py's main()/module body is a known limitation of the EntryPoint model (built for request-handler style entries) and does not reflect reality for a console_scripts CLI entry point that setup.cfg proves is the actual invocation path."
          },
