        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-601",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "_http_get (used by scan_url and scan_huggingface_model to fetch attacker/third-party-supplied URLs, e.g. a Hugging Face repo's sibling file list and model binaries) follows HTTP 302 redirects by recursively calling `_http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])` with no cap on redirect count and no check that the Location host/scheme matches an expected allowlist (e.g. still huggingface.co, still https). A malicious or compromised server can redirect indefinitely (unbounded recursion / DoS) or redirect the scanner to an arbitrary attacker-chosen host, and picklescan will blindly follow it.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived directly from source. `_http_get` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:240-259) is the single HTTP fetch primitive used by both `scan_url` (line 611: `scan_bytes(io.BytesIO(_http_get(url)), url)`, where `url` is a fully caller/attacker-supplied argument) and `scan_huggingface_model` (lines 561 and 572: `_http_get(f\"https://huggingface.co/{repo_id}/resolve/main/{file_name}\")`, fetching attacker-influenced Hugging Face model binaries). On a 302 response, the function recurses with `return _http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])` (line 252) \u2014 the Location header value is passed straight back into `_http_get` with zero validation: no redirect-count/depth cap (no counter, no max-redirect parameter anywhere in the function or file), and no check that the new URL's scheme/host matches the original `parsed_url.netloc`/scheme (the only two uses of `netloc`/`scheme` in the file are building the *initial* connection and formatting the error message on line 255, neither of which compares against the Location header). I verified this with a direct grep across the whole file for `redirect|netloc|scheme|allowlist|Location` and found no comparison logic at all. The graph corroborates reachability: `_http_get` has `reachable_from_entry=true`, `hop_distance=0`. Net effect: a malicious/compromised server (or a MITM/compromised CDN in the redirect chain) can (a) redirect indefinitely causing unbounded recursion (stack-exhaustion DoS), or (b) redirect the scanner to an arbitrary attacker-chosen scheme/host, and `_http_get` will blindly follow and fetch from it, then feed the result into the pickle/zip scanner \u2014 a classic unvalidated-redirect / open-redirect-follow issue (CWE-601), with a secondary unbounded-recursion DoS angle (CWE-674)."
          },
