        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-400",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "_http_get opens `http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc)` with no `timeout=` argument, so a malicious or slow remote host (reached via scan_url/scan_huggingface_model) can hold the connection open indefinitely and hang the scan \u2014 there is no connection/read timeout control anywhere in the networking code.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read the actual source: `_http_get` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:240-259) opens `http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc)` at line 246 with only the netloc positional argument \u2014 no `timeout=` kwarg. A package-wide grep for \"timeout\" and \"signal.alarm\" across all of src/picklescan returns zero matches, confirming no timeout is set anywhere (not in this function, not via `socket.setdefaulttimeout()`, not via an outer watchdog/signal). `HTTPSConnection` without an explicit timeout defaults to blocking indefinitely (no OS-level bound), so `conn.request()`/`conn.getresponse()` at lines 248-249 can hang forever against a slow or non-responding/malicious remote host. Graph confirms this is reachable from real entry points: `src/picklescan/scanner.py:<module>._http_get` has `reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0`, and both its callers `scan_url` (line 608\u2192611) and `scan_huggingface_model` (lines 557, 561, 572) are likewise `reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0` \u2014 i.e. these are themselves top-level library entry points that take attacker-influenced input (an arbitrary URL / HF repo_id) and feed it straight into the unbounded HTTPS call. Note: the redirect-following recursive call at line 252 (`_http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])`) makes the lack of a per-request timeout even more consequential, since an attacker-controlled redirect chain compounds the hang risk. This matches the analyst's claim; I only correct/tighten the cited range to the whole function body."
          },
