        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "scan_7z_bytes extracts attacker-supplied archive members by name from an untrusted .7z file (`archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets)` where targets come straight from `archive.getnames()` filtered only by file-extension suffix) and then re-derives the on-disk path via `os.path.join(tmpdir, file_name)` to open/scan it \u2014 picklescan's own code never normalizes/validates that the resulting path stays inside tmpdir (no realpath+prefix check, no rejection of '..' or absolute member names) before extracting/opening it, i.e. the standard archive-extraction containment check is absent in-repo.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read the real source directly and independently re-derived the claim. In src/picklescan/scanner.py, scan_7z_bytes (lines 415-436) filters 7z archive member names from archive.getnames() purely by extension suffix (line 426: `f.endswith(tuple(_pickle_file_extensions))`), passes those attacker-controlled names straight to `archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets)` (line 429), then independently reconstructs the on-disk path with `file_path = os.path.join(tmpdir, file_name)` (line 431) and opens/scans it via `scan_file_path(file_path)` (line 434, which does `open(path, \"rb\")` at line 604). No normalization/containment check (no realpath, no commonpath/abspath-prefix check, no rejection of '..' or absolute member names) exists anywhere in this function or in scan_file_path \u2014 confirmed by reading the full function body end-to-end. This is a real, independently-exploitable pattern even setting aside py7zr's own extraction safety: in Python, `os.path.join(tmpdir, file_name)` silently discards `tmpdir` and returns `file_name` verbatim whenever `file_name` is an absolute path, and relative `../` components are preserved un-normalized \u2014 so a crafted 7z member name ending in one of the whitelisted suffixes (.pkl/.pickle/.joblib/.dat/.data) that is absolute or contains traversal segments causes `os.path.isfile`/`open` at line 433-434 to resolve outside tmpdir, i.e., CWE-22. (The orchestrator's requirements.txt pins py7zr==0.22.0; I could not inspect py7zr's own extract()-path sanitization since the package isn't vendored/installed in this environment and the exploit-reference corpus is unindexed here, so whether archive.extract() can also be made to *write* outside tmpdir during extraction remains unverified \u2014 but the read-side containment gap in picklescan's own code stands regardless of that.) Reachability is strongly confirmed, not just plausible: querying the graph shows every call in lines 429-434 has reachable_from_entry=true, and scan_7z_bytes itself is registered as an EntryPoint/handler with hop_distance=0 (it is a public API function reachable directly, and is also called from scan_bytes at scanner.py:508/552, which is invoked from the public scan_file_path/scan_url/scan_directory_path entry points) \u2014 exactly the \"scan an untrusted file\" usage this security tool exists for. Note the asymmetry with the adjacent scan_zip_bytes (line 439+), which reads ZIP members in-memory via `zip.open(file_name)` and never calls `zip.extract()` to disk, so it has no analogous disk-path reconstruction step \u2014 underscoring that the 7z path specifically lacks the containment check the analyst flagged."
          },
